{
  "section": "consensus-coordination",
  "addedResources": [],
  "units": [
    {
      "id": "state-machine-replication-safety-liveness",
      "title": "State-machine replication and safety versus liveness",
      "hours": 3,
      "target": [
        "explain"
      ],
      "topics": [
        "state-machine-replication-model",
        "safety-versus-liveness"
      ],
      "outcomes": [
        "Explain how an agreed command prefix and deterministic execution produce replicated state.",
        "Separate election safety, log matching and state-machine safety from conditional progress.",
        "State the crash-recovery, storage and timing assumptions behind your consensus contract."
      ],
      "why": "Build on Replication, quorums and consensus vocabulary. Here the contract becomes a ledger of properties that every later Raft transition must preserve, not another survey of replication styles.",
      "next": [
        {
          "unit": "distributed-systems/consensus-coordination/raft-terms-roles-timeouts",
          "note": "Turn the contract into election transitions."
        }
      ],
      "study": [
        {
          "resource": "raft-paper-ds5",
          "title": "Raft extended paper (20 May 2014): replication contract",
          "url": "https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf",
          "locator": "Section 2, Replicated state machines, and Figure 1; Figure 3, Raft guarantees; Section 5.6, Timing and availability",
          "depth": "Read the guarantees as obligations to justify later. Skip the Paxos comparison and the evaluation for now."
        },
        {
          "resource": "mit-raft-lab-ds5",
          "title": "MIT 6.5840 Spring 2026 Lab 3: module boundary",
          "url": "https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.824/labs/lab-raft1.html",
          "locator": "Introduction, Getting Started and The code, through the description of `labrpc`",
          "depth": "Inspect the interface and public tests. Begin implementation in the election unit; keep course solutions private."
        }
      ],
      "focus": [
        "Replicas at different applied indexes need not have identical current state. Equal initial state and an equal deterministic command prefix imply equal state at that prefix.",
        "Uncommitted suffixes may differ. State-machine safety forbids different commands being applied at the same index, not temporary differences between whole logs.",
        "Basic Raft safety assumes correct protocol execution and stable storage across crashes, not bounded message delay. Progress also needs a communicating majority and sufficiently stable timing and leadership.",
        "A safety counterexample has a finite bad prefix. A bounded interval without commits is evidence of a stall, not a proof of permanent nontermination."
      ],
      "reproduce": "Closed-book, draw the client, consensus module, log and state machine from Figure 1. Trace an append through local acceptance, durable replication, commitment, application and reply. Mark which boundaries the Lab 3 interface exposes and which belong to the service. Classify a conflicting applied command as a safety violation and a minority partition with no completed writes as a progress stall.",
      "traps": [
        "Treating `Start()` returning as a successful client write. It starts agreement; it does not promise commitment.",
        "Putting local randomness or wall-clock reads inside the deterministic application transition instead of agreeing their results as command data.",
        "Reading the paper's availability summary as an unconditional termination theorem for any network with a connected majority. Timing and competing elections still matter."
      ],
      "selfTest": [
        {
          "q": "Two replicas have applied different-length prefixes. Must their current states match?",
          "a": "No. Compare state after the same prefix. A lagging replica can be safe while behind."
        },
        {
          "q": "Can a safe log contain different commands at one index on different servers?",
          "a": "Yes, in uncommitted suffixes. They must not both become applied decisions at that index."
        },
        {
          "q": "Does a timeout establish that the leader crashed?",
          "a": "No. It creates suspicion. Safety must survive false suspicion; progress needs additional timing and communication assumptions."
        }
      ],
      "practice": [],
      "practiceNote": "The section mini-project covers this: the `loglab` invariant ledger, fault-model statement and separate acceptance, persistence, commit, apply and reply events.",
      "stopWhen": "Your ledger names a finite witness for each safety property and states the assumptions needed for progress without requiring identical uncommitted logs.",
      "fit": {
        "verdict": "kept",
        "note": "The paper supplies the precise contract and timing boundary; the lab interface and mini-project make the abstraction boundaries observable."
      }
    },
